  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Metformin is the traditional first-choice medication for type 2 diabetes, but modern medical guidelines recommend choosing SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists as early or first-line therapy when patients have specific heart, kidney, or weight-loss goals.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "SGLT2 inhibitors",
            "link": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10455499/"
          },
          {
            "text": "GLP-1 receptor agonists",
            "link": "https://www.goodrx.com/conditions/diabetes-type-2/glp-1-vs-sglt2"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "modern medical guidelines recommend choosing SGLT2 inhibitors or GLP-1 receptor agonists as early or first-line therapy when patients have specific heart, kidney, or weight-loss goals"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Work"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors (such as empagliflozin/Jardiance) block a protein in your kidneys. This makes your body pass extra sugar out through your urine. They come as daily oral pills."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 receptor agonists (such as semaglutide/Ozempic) mimic a natural gut hormone. They help your pancreas release insulin, slow down digestion, and lower your appetite. Most are weekly injections, though oral forms exist."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Blood Sugar and Weight Effects"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Sugar (HbA1c): GLP-1 medications typically lower A1C by 1.0% to 2.0%. SGLT2 inhibitors lower A1C by about 0.5% to 1.0%."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Weight Loss: GLP-1 drugs cause greater weight loss (averaging 4 to 6 kg). SGLT2 inhibitors cause modest weight loss (1 to 3 kg)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "When to Choose SGLT2 Inhibitors"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "You have heart failure. SGLT2 drugs protect the heart and reduce hospital visits for heart failure."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "You have chronic kidney disease. They slow down kidney damage."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "You prefer taking a daily pill instead of an injection."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "When to Choose GLP-1 Agonists"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Weight management is your main goal alongside blood sugar control."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "You need a higher risk reduction for heart attacks and strokes (atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Side Effects to Consider"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 inhibitors: Higher risk of urinary tract infections or genital yeast infections."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 agonists: Stomach upset, nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help you understand which option fits best, please tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have heart disease , heart failure, or kidney issues?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Is weight loss a top priority for you?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you strongly prefer a pill over an injection?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
